# Session logs contain the full, unredacted contents of every tool call and
# every tool result: file contents, API responses, tokens the agent happened to
# read. Treat them like a password file. Never commit them.
*.jsonl

# ...except the golden fixture, which is test data rather than a recording.
# Without this the pattern above swallowed it, and the golden test skipped
# itself in CI: present, green, and checking nothing.
!corpus/golden/*.jsonl

# The benign corpus is real recorded traffic and stays out of the repository.
# The attack corpus is synthetic fixture data and is committed, so it is not
# ignored here.
/corpus/benign/

# Build output. Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored "mcp-guard" also
# matches the cmd/mcp-guard/ directory and silently drops the entry point.
/dist/
/mcp-guard
/mcp-guard.exe

# Working notes, kept on disk and out of the published repository.
#
# CLAUDE.md is context for an agent working in this directory. ROADMAP tracks
# item state against the plan. docs/ holds the original design brief, which is
# written in Russian and addresses its reader personally — including career
# plans that were never meant to be public.
#
# None of it is secret, all of it is internal, and a repository someone else
# clones should be the tool rather than the notebook it was built from.
CLAUDE.md
ROADMAP.md
ROADMAP_ru.md
/docs/
/.claude/

# Baselines and locks produced by running the tool against this machine.
#
# This is not advice to leave yours uncommitted - the whole point of a lock file
# is that it sits in the repository it guards, so a changed tool or a rewritten
# config shows up in review. It is about which repository. A baseline scoped to
# a *project* covers that project's .cursor/mcp.json and belongs in it. One
# scoped to a *machine* covers %APPDATA%\Claude and ~/.claude.json, and records
# the directory layout and the list of servers its owner runs.
#
# Anything generated here is the second kind, produced while testing, so it is
# ignored rather than published.
/mcp-guard.configs.lock
/mcp-guard.lock
